<SectionComponent tag="h1" 
  id="customization-1"
  bind:intersecting={visibleById["customization-1"]}
  onIntersectChange={(visible) => setVisible("customization-1", visible)}
>Customization</SectionComponent>
<p>From the five parts of a language definition, Freon generates a complete work environment containing an editor,
scope provider (scoper), type provider (typer), validator, standard library, and utilities such as a parser and deparser.
These individual components are collectively referred to as <strong><em>fre-tools</em></strong>.
Other sections of this documentation describe how to fine-tune the generated fre-tools using the files in the <a
  href="/Documentation/Overview/Getting_Started#creating-a-project-2"
><em>defs</em> folder</a>
(i.e. the folder containing your definition files).
(See for instance <a
  href="/Documentation/Defining_an_Editor"
>Defining_an_editor</a>.)
In this section, we focus on deeper customizations implemented through TypeScript code.</p>
<SectionComponent tag="h2" 
  id="stacked-architecture-2"
  bind:intersecting={visibleById["stacked-architecture-2"]}
  onIntersectChange={(visible) => setVisible("stacked-architecture-2", visible)}
>Stacked Architecture</SectionComponent>
<p>Customization is made possible by Freon’s <em>stacked architecture</em>.
This architecture makes it possible to create your language,
and customize it step-by-step in an agile fashion.
Freon’s design philosophy can be summarized by the following quote from
the well-known graphical user interface designer Alan Kay:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>Simple things should be simple, complex things should be possible.</em>
(<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Kay" target="_blank">Alan Kay, Turing Award Winner</a>)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>(Read our <a href="/Background">Background</a> to understand why we have chosen this architecture.)
Freon supports three levels of customization.</p>
<ol>
<li>The default level, which is always available.</li>
<li>The definition level, where the fre-tools are being generated based on the input from the definition files (e.g. a <code>.scope</code> file).</li>
<li>The customization level, where handwritten TypeScript code can be added or used to replace parts of the generated output.</li>
</ol>
<p>Freon aims to provide around 80% of the required functionality of the work environment through the first two levels.</p>
<Figure 
imageName={'documentation/layered-architecture2.png'} 
caption={'The Stacked Architecture'}
figureNumber={1}
/>
<p>Freon combines the definitions for each tool, together with the customized TypeScript code into one application,
where the third level precedes the second, and the second level precedes the first.
For example, the generated editor determines for each <a
  href="/Documentation/Creating_the_Metamodel/Language_Structure#concept-4"
><strong>concept</strong></a> in the language which projection to use, in the following order:</p>
<ol>
<li>use the <em>hand-written projection</em> from the Customization level, when this is present. If not, the editor will</li>
<li>use the <em>projection generated from the .edit definition</em> from the Freon definition Level, when this definition is present.
Finally, when no definition is present, the editor will</li>
<li>fall back to the <em>default projection</em>, the one generated when no <code>.edit</code> definition file is present.</li>
</ol>
<Figure
imageName={'documentation/fall-through.png'}
caption={'Projection Lookup for an AST Node'}
figureNumber={2}
/>
<p>This layered approach allows language engineers to start quickly with a working environment and
refine it gradually at either the definition or customization level.
For this purpose,
the <a
  href="/Documentation/Overview/Getting_Started#the-command-line-interface-9"
>Command Line Interface</a> includes separate commands
for generating the different fre-tools.</p>
